My quick assessment of the problem:

When choosing to clobber existing assets, we make an index of any old assets whose file name matches a name of the new asset to be uploaded: https://github.com/cli/cli/blob/f7930a430b394f4c6ac0223f9f1ae269763cbd1c/pkg/cmd/release/upload/upload.go#L95-L99

All this is good when asset names are simple filenames, i.e. only contain characters in the Latin alphabet plus a few glyphs like `.` or `-` that are common in file names. However, any other "special" characters in the asset name (like a space) are going to get replaced on the platform when the asset is uploaded. Here is what the platform does in its `sanitize_file_name` function, roughly:
- Transliterates Unicode glyphs to their Latin alphabet equivalents;
- Prefixes file names that start with `.` with `default.`;
- Replaces all `/[^a-z0-9\-_\+@]+/i` characters to `.`;
- Replaces all consecutive runs of multiple `.` with a single `.` and trims leading and trailing `.`s.

I think that in order for us to check for existing assets to clobber, we'll have to perform asset name transformation on the client as it would be done on the server. This is tricky and risks falling out of date with the server implementation, but luckily doesn't have to be perfect to cover most cases.

A perfect solution would be that the platform API gains an ability to clobber an existing asset, so that we don't have to do any of this logic on the client. However we can't count on a new platform feature just yet.
